Z||000009||Jefrysuko||09-17-2001||01:49 AM||||Ok guys here is the scoop.  The Fiero does not have a posi traction differential or anything for that matter to prevent one tire spinning while the other doesn't.  Why is it that when you spin your tires you get two black marks?  Since the Fiero doesn't have input into the differential at 90 degrees to the drive axles there is not an induced torque that makes an imbalance of downforce on the rear wheels.<P>In other words unless you are on uneven or different roadsurfaces between the two rear wheels you shouldn't notice one tire spinning and the other not.<P>As far as putting a posi or limited slip differential in a Fiero I do believe it is possible.  I remember someone advertising one before but it was close to $1000.  Also by using something like this you will drastically alter the driving characteristics of the car and might even make it so that you tend to break the tires loose every time you make a turn.<P>Jeffrey D Suckow<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/halfpole.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Joe Torma||09-17-2001||10:08 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Jefrysuko:<BR><B>might even make it so that you tend to break the tires loose every time you make a turn.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Sounds like fun!!! Check out Quaffe.  They have posi units for I think the Izuzus.<P>Would this decrease 0-60 & 1/4 mi times?<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/simplesig1.gif">   <BR>Did you get your car in my book yet????<BR> <A HREF="http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm" TARGET=_blank>http://www.animatedconcepts.com/fierobook.htm</A><p>[This message has been edited by Joe Torma (edited 09-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000011||Ambush||09-17-2001||10:29 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Joe Torma:<BR><B> Sounds like fun!!! Check out Quaffe.  They have posi units for I think the Izuzus.<P>Would this decrease 0-60 & 1/4 mi?<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Posi shoud increase 1/4 mile times, but only if you have enough power to slip the rear. If there isn't enough power then the posi would slow you down do to the little added weight.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Turbo88||09-17-2001||10:53 AM||||Well the positive traction differential (posi) won't add any negligable weight. A posi differential and a regular differential are fundimentally the same. Generally, GM posi units use small clutches inside the differential to assist in more equal transmission of power to the tires. The clutches will slip around corners which allows your tires to turn at different speeds. As far as weight, the posi units might weight a few ounces more than non-posi units. The reason you leave two black marks is there is only a nominal difference in available traction between your tires. But, when you launch, as your traction changes, one tire might bite a little harder than the other which will bias most the torque to other tire. This is often the case at the drag strip when you see someone spinning out of the burnout box and it seems they are switching between tires. The burning slick gets hotter and sticks better. So it bite harder. If the motor has enough power, it will start burning the other slick until the process repeates itself. This is generally bad because you are sending a lot of torque shock out through your axles. Tends to break things. Although most Fieros are not running in the power level. Those that are, are probably slipping the clutch anyway (the clutches on fieros are tiny). Automatics are a little different. If the converter locks, you are pushing power straight through the transaxle (I can't remember if the th125s in the fieros are locking types or not). <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000013||Formula88||09-17-2001||11:48 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Turbo88:<BR><B>Those that are, are probably slipping the clutch anyway (the clutches on fieros are tiny). Automatics are a little different. If the converter locks, you are pushing power straight through the transaxle (I can't remember if the th125s in the fieros are locking types or not). </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Clutch or auto doesn't matter.  We're talking differential here (tranny output), not the input into the tranny.  <P>No. Fiero's don't have Posi. <BR>Yes. You can get it, for big $$$.<BR>It probably won't make much, if any, difference in your car's performance unless you have a LOT of mods and are putting out some major power.  <P>------------------<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><I><FONT COLOR="blue">The path of the righteous man is beset on all sides by the inequities of the selfish and the tyrranys of evil men. Blessed is he who in the name of charity and good will shepards the weak through the valley of darkness for he is truly his brothers keeper, and the finder of lost children. And I shall strike down upon thee with great vengence and furious anger, those who attempt to poison and destroy my brothers. And you will know my name is the lord when I lay my vengence upon thee. </I></FONT></FONT><BR>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/9-11-01a.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Turbo88||09-17-2001||03:16 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula88:<BR><B> Clutch or auto doesn't matter.  We're talking differential here (tranny output), not the input into the tranny.  <P>No. Fiero's don't have Posi. <BR>Yes. You can get it, for big $$$.<BR>It probably won't make much, if any, difference in your car's performance unless you have a LOT of mods and are putting out some major power.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yes I understand that the transaxle doesn't matter as far as having a posi. my point is saying that was explaining the differences in torque shock and engine power and how it relates to the use of the differential.. <P>You can get a posi differential for a fiero from <A HREF="http://www.isuzuperformance.com" TARGET=_blank>www.isuzuperformance.com</A>  which I believe makes a unit for both the getrag and isuzu designs. However, they are very expensive and probably not worth it.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Dave Gunsul||09-17-2001||04:06 PM||||Any loss of traction is lost time, or gained<BR>time on your 1/4 mile times really, at the strip so the posi would make a difference especially in stick shift cars. Autos have a<BR>tuffer time breaking lose so they wouldn't benefit as much from the posi. (note that<BR>i said as much for those of you that are putting out a lot of power in autos)<BR>BUT: as someone already stated above there<BR>is a greater chance of breakage since all,<BR>or at least more, of your power is "hooking up". <P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver<p>[This message has been edited by Dave Gunsul (edited 09-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||Turbo88||09-17-2001||04:34 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Dave Gunsul:<BR><B>Any loss of traction is lost time, or gained<BR>time on your 1/4 mile times really, at the strip so the posi would make a difference especially in stick shift cars. Autos have a<BR>tuffer time breaking lose so they wouldn't benefit as much from the posi. (note that<BR>i said as much for those of you that are putting out a lot of power in autos)<BR>BUT: as someone already stated above there<BR>is a greater chance of breakage since all,<BR>or at least more, of your power is "hooking up". <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That is sort of true. If you bite so hard that it bogs your engine (ie you don't have the bottom end torque or enough stall converter), then you will run slower. An example is, I cut a 13.71 in my 99 Trans Am on Goodyear RS-As (not the best bighting tire). I put on a friends ET Streets and got no spin anywhere but never even made it into 3rd gear (4 speed overdrive automatic). I cut a 14.08. I changed the tires back and cut a 13.67. In the majority of Fieros out there (peppier v8s excluded) I would say a little wheel spin off the line is good to let the motor come into it's power band. Most decent drag strips are level enough and have even enough traction to allow you to use power on both wheels on launch. On burnout you will notice it spinning one tire at a time. ||||reg||1
Z||000017||Dave Gunsul||09-17-2001||11:10 PM||||While I agree with what turbo88 just wrote above it seems that I didn't make myself clear on what I was saying. What I meant was:<BR>If you burn out when leaving the line in a race you will run slower in the 1/4 because you're just sitting there spinning and not moving, or moving slower than you could be.<BR>This is also true on the street, even more true in fact, since there's no traction/tire<BR>grip stuff on the ground to help keep your tire stuck to the ground.<BR>The only time that this is not true is when you have a situation like the one that turbo88 wrote above.<BR>Sorry for any confusion.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000018||Turbo88||09-18-2001||12:47 AM||||I know what ya ment <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. I was just trying to clear up my point. From my experience, any time you are on a level surface with consistant traction variables you will tend to get a 50-50% distabution of torque (avaliable traction doesn't really matter as long as it's split equally. Less = longer black marks). I agree with Dave in that God only knows what puddle of crap the beater in front of you left behind and will cause variations on the road. But then again, none of us ever street race anyway, right..  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">..<P><BR>Ya.. Right!||||reg||1
